Hi, everyone.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:13.860
I am Binal Shah, Networking
Specialist at Google.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.720
I work with other customers with
their networking requirements

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.870
and architecture
on Google Cloud.

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:24.620
So, today, we're going
to talk about security.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:26.330
You know, security--
network security

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:28.970
is a big part of
infrastructure design.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:31.400
You know, whether you are
deploying your environment

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.890
on prem or in cloud, right?

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:37.290
There is a requirement
to secure your resources.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:41.000
Today, we will talk about
some of the best practices

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:46.490
and how you secure the
environment in Google Cloud.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.920
So first thing you would
start with, whenever

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.380
you start deploying a
project, first thing you do

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.750
is spin up a VPC.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.530
By definition, VPC
is an isolated domain

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:01.640
where no one has access to
it unless you allow access.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:05.450
So as you're adding users
and assigning them IAM roles,

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:10.610
a good practice is to follow the
principle of least privilege.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:13.520
So understand a user's
function and then only

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.970
allow as much access the
user needs to do their job.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:20.240
This way you reduce errors
due to this configuration

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.542
and thereby reduce downtime.

00:02:21.542 --> 00:02:23.000
STEPHANIE WONG: So
this is relating

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.990
to IAM policies, Identity and
Access Management permissions

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:27.816
and roles.

00:02:27.816 --> 00:02:28.940
BINAL SHAH: That's correct.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:32.360
And then you start deploying
instances in your VPC.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.820
At that point, you
want to restrict flows

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:35.870
to your instances.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:37.730
That's how you protect
your instances.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:41.270
For this, you use
firewall rules.

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:44.810
You know, we'll talk a little
bit more about firewall rules,

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:46.580
but this is a good time--

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:49.130
before you start spinning
up your instances, right--

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:51.560
it's a good time to sit
down with your application

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:54.470
team and network and
security teams together

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.504
to identify what flows the
application needs, right?

00:02:57.504 --> 00:02:59.420
You do want to make sure
the application runs,

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:02.180
but at the same time, you
want to protect your instances

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.841
from unwanted traffic.

00:03:04.841 --> 00:03:07.700
All right, now you
have your VPC set up.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:10.950
The next thing you want to do
is connect back to on prem.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:14.100
So we have a few ways
we are able to do this.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.720
So let's say you want to
talk to your Google services,

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.100
like G Suite.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.200
So in this case, from
on prem to G Suite,

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.930
you can deploy
direct peering, where

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:27.320
you peer your router with
one of Google's routers

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.580
in the process peering location.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.450
And then Google is your next
hub for all the Google services

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:34.040
traffic.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.680
This way, given that
Google is your next hub,

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:38.270
you're not going
over the internet,

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.660
crossing multiple ASNs, so
that reduces your latency

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:43.610
and gives you
better performance.

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:45.784
Now, you also want
to have connectivity

00:03:45.784 --> 00:03:48.200
between your servers, and you
want private IP connectivity

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.400
between this, right?

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:50.410
Couple options, right?

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:51.950
You can do cloud VPN.

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:55.420
Cloud VPN is our
site-to-site IPsec VPN.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:57.690
The traffic goes
over the internet,

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:59.460
but it is fully encrypted.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.160
So it is still secure.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:04.490
Cloud VPN is a good solution
when you have lower bandwidth

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:08.450
requirement, and it's very
quick to set up and get going.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:10.880
Now, we do have customers--
sometimes they do not

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.880
want to let their traffic
go over the internet at all,

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:15.830
or they have very large
volumes of traffic,

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:18.589
so we're talking about
tens of gigs of data.

00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:21.320
So for this we have dedicated
interconnect options.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.350
So dedicated interconnect,
again, you connect your on prem

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:26.240
and meet us at one
of the core locations

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.280
and connect directly
to Google Cloud.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:31.610
From there, you put a
VLAN attachment and cloud

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.080
router in your VPC.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.780
So you have connectivity
directly into your VPC.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:38.840
And then between the cloud
router and your on prem router,

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.905
you link local BGP
to exchange routes.

00:04:40.905 --> 00:04:43.280
STEPHANIE WONG: So, again,
this is internal communication

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:43.880
across.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:45.713
BINAL SHAH: That is
internal communications.

00:04:45.713 --> 00:04:49.100
Your servers will talk using
private IPs, so 10 10, 10 1,

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:53.360
can talk to 190 to
160, for example.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:53.860
Great.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:55.870
So we have the
connectivity set up.

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:59.200
Now, oftentimes, we
see customers come in.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.090
They have an
architecture on prem,

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:04.870
and they're using a lot
of vendor solutions,

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.840
like next gen
firewall ideas, IBS.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.800
And they would like to
carry that architecture

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:11.710
into Google Cloud, right?

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:14.930
By way of our partner solutions,
they are able to do that.

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:16.900
So they can work
with their partners

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:19.920
and deploy the same solutions in
their Google Cloud environment.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:20.711
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:05:20.711 --> 00:05:23.140
So just to reiterate, you
are able to use third party

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:25.150
appliances and bring
them over to GCP.

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:25.630
BINAL SHAH: Absolutely.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:26.920
STEPHANIE WONG: That way,
we're able to kind of meet you

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:27.503
where you are.

00:05:27.503 --> 00:05:30.620
You can continue to
re-architect in the same way.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:32.260
BINAL SHAH: Yes.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.450
You know, always look
at what Google offers,

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:37.870
and then, like I said,
customers have their solutions.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.870
They also want to work with
the same vendors that they are,

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:42.850
then we have that capability
in our marketplace

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:46.260
so you can find those vendors.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:50.800
So now you deployed the
applications in Google Cloud,

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:51.780
and you want to scale.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:54.250
So you're going to front
it with a load balancer.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:56.710
So Google's Global
Load Balancers

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:01.090
provide you edge protection
from L3 and L4 attacks.

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:05.410
So Google's Global Load
Balancer also uses anycast IP--

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:08.510
that is one IP
advertised globally.

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:13.180
So you can front end your
application with just one IP.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.810
And you remember the Google's
global infrastructure, right?

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:20.200
Just by the way of the
infrastructure that we have,

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:22.360
customers are able
to have back ends

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:26.230
in different regions
behind their load balances.

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:29.500
So what this does is it
brings the workloads closer

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.810
to the user, so
your end user will

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:37.171
see less latency and, hence,
improved performance, right?

00:06:37.171 --> 00:06:39.670
STEPHANIE WONG: You mentioned
that this Global Load Balancer

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:41.560
offers L3, L4 protection.

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:43.210
Are there any other
additional ways

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.460
that you're able to
protect your applications?

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:46.418
BINAL SHAH: Absolutely.

00:06:46.418 --> 00:06:50.230
So we have a product
called Cloud Armor, which

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:51.820
integrates with
the load balancer

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:53.920
and provides
additional protection

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:57.340
from DDOS and for the
applications, right?

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:00.850
In addition, we also have,
again, partner solutions

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:01.950
that customers can use.

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:03.700
Again, customers can
be already using it,

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:07.330
and they want more protection
for the-- specific protection

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:08.290
for the application.

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:13.390
Then these solutions are
still available to use.

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:15.570
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:17.820
BINAL SHAH: So now let's
take a little bit deeper dive

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:20.520
into some of these
solutions, right?

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.030
So when you spin up
instances in your VPC,

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:26.610
first thing you're going to
do is give it a private IP,

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:30.330
and then you have an
option to add a public IP.

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:33.690
Once you assign a public IP
to an instance, remember,

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:37.540
this instance is accessible
from the rest of the world.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:40.110
So if your instance
does not need

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.480
to be reachable
from the outside,

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.350
you don't need to
add a public IP.

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:48.970
This way, you just reduce the
attack surface, for instance.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:52.980
Now, there are cases where your
instance can have a private IP,

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:55.020
but it still needs
to go out, right?

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:56.500
It needs to get
updates, et cetera.

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:59.160
So it will need
outbound communication.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:01.620
This we enable using Cloud NAT.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:05.250
So Cloud NAT is Google's
software defined solution,

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:06.930
so this is not a VM.

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:08.490
This is not an appliance.

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:12.510
It's just a scalable
software solution.

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:15.960
It is regional, so you add
this Cloud NAT in each region.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:18.390
Then all of the subnets
within that region

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:20.710
can use this NAT to go out.

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:23.560
You also have control over if
you only want a specific subnet

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:24.060
to go out.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:27.280
You may still not want some
instances to go out at all,

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:29.132
so you do have that
kind of control.

00:08:29.132 --> 00:08:30.840
STEPHANIE WONG: So we
also have customers

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.610
who might have
been used to using

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:36.720
other architectural setups,
like bastion hosts or proxies.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:38.820
In addition to having
this Cloud NAT option,

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:41.628
when do you recommend
each kind of use case?

00:08:41.628 --> 00:08:43.169
BINAL SHAH: Good
question, Stephanie.

00:08:43.169 --> 00:08:45.290
So let's say I
have my instances,

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:48.300
and I need to SSH
into these, right?

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:51.480
I don't want to open SSH
to the entire world, right?

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:55.290
I know who needs to access, so I
can allow just my corporate IPs

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:56.130
to access.

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:58.170
But usually what
do you do, this is

00:08:58.170 --> 00:08:59.440
where you use a bastion host.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.140
So you keep one bastion host.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.600
You allow access to that
host, and these instances

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:05.870
can only be accessed
from the bastion host.

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:09.390
That way, that is a lot
more controlled access.

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:09.930
Right?

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:12.450
And you can also use
proxies to go out.

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:14.319
A lot of customers use that.

00:09:14.319 --> 00:09:15.860
And one more thing
I wanted to note--

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:18.715
so if you wanted to talk
to Google services--

00:09:18.715 --> 00:09:21.300
so your instances wanted
to reach Google services,

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:24.840
like Cloud Storage
or BigQuery, they

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.590
can do that using private IP.

00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:29.070
So you don't need to
add a public IP just

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:30.330
to access Google services.

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:30.570
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:31.110
That's great.

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:32.080
BINAL SHAH: And
that's pretty neat.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:32.670
Yes.

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:33.300
Awesome.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:35.620
Thank you.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:38.700
So the next step is you
want to restrict flows

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:39.700
to your instance, right?

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:42.070
You want to protect
your instances.

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:44.500
We have Google's
distributed firewalls

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:45.850
that will help with this.

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:47.100
How are they different, right?

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.760
It's important to understand.

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:51.310
On the left side, you
see an architecture

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:53.590
that you traditionally
see on prem, right?

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:56.050
So the firewalls are
closer to the edge.

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:57.490
All traffic comes in.

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:00.250
And here's this firewall squared
out through these firewalls.

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:03.970
Sometimes, firewalls
can be a choking point,

00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:08.290
but more importantly, once that
traffic leaves the firewall,

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:10.450
there is little
control, no easy way

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:13.960
to control communication
between instances.

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:19.420
So on Google Cloud, our firewall
is a distributed firewall,

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:21.820
and the way it works
is, when a packet

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:24.550
is trying to reach an
instance, the firewall rules

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:26.950
are checked at that
time, and only if there's

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:30.780
a firewall rule that allows
this traffic to the instance

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:32.406
will that packet be allowed.

00:10:32.406 --> 00:10:34.030
If there's no firewall
rule, the packet

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:37.035
does not reach that instance.

00:10:37.035 --> 00:10:38.410
So as you see in
the diagram, you

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:41.290
can control communication
north-south, as well as

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:42.220
east-west.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:44.440
So if you look at
this diagram, there

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:47.350
are instances with a
tag, called red tag,

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:49.910
and some instances
with a blue tag.

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:52.030
So when I add my
firewall rule, my rule

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:55.580
will look like source
will be red tag--

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:56.890
ends with red tag--

00:10:56.890 --> 00:10:58.990
and destination
will be blue tags.

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:03.280
This is a neat way to group
together similar function VMs.

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:06.360
And what happens is
you've now enabled

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:08.230
east-west communication.

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:11.890
Another option is to use
service accounts on VM.

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:14.860
That's a bit more
secure than tags,

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:18.190
because, with tags, it
needs compute admin--

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:21.520
it still needs compute admin
privilege to change the tag.

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:24.530
That can be done while
the VM is running.

00:11:24.530 --> 00:11:27.010
So depending on your use
case, you have options.

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:28.000
STEPHANIE WONG: I see.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:29.800
And for the use
case of those who

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:31.270
have projects in
different regions

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:33.610
or just have shared
VPC, and they

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:36.070
want internal communication
across multiple projects,

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:38.914
how would you set up firewalls
or communication across them?

00:11:38.914 --> 00:11:39.580
BINAL SHAH: Yes.

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:43.930
So a firewall rule is
global within a VPC, right?

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:46.840
So if I have one VPC and
have multiple regions,

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.120
there's still going to be
one firewall rule stable.

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:51.790
You add your rules there.

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:54.440
Now, you mentioned the
case of shared VPC.

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:58.760
So in shared VPC, case there's
one VPC in a host project,

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:01.480
and now you are connecting
a lot of service projects

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:05.480
and sharing these subnets with
the different service projects.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:06.970
So it's a valid point.

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:08.710
You want to control
communication

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:10.510
between these projects, right?

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:13.900
So you still go back
in the host project

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:17.390
and in those shared VPC, and
add firewall rules there.

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:19.660
So shared VPC is
still like a VPC.

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:22.000
You have the ability to
share your subnets down.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:25.810
So I would say subnet 1
shared with service project 1,

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:29.380
and subnet 2 shared with service
project 2, I would put a rule--

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:31.990
so much communication on
these boards, these protocols,

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:35.065
to these VMs is allowed
between these two projects.

00:12:37.611 --> 00:12:38.110
Cool.

00:12:38.110 --> 00:12:42.890
By default, all
ingress will be denied.

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:47.230
So only allow-- again, another
principle is only allow access

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:48.280
that you need to.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:50.500
By default, egress
is all allowed,

00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:52.150
but there may be
cases where customers

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:55.810
do want to restrict that, so
you have the ability to do that.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:02.450
And here is an example
of how firewall rules can

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:05.840
work together in Google Cloud.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:08.840
Like we said before, before
you deploy applications,

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:11.240
it's a very good idea
to know the flows.

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:13.040
Couple of things,
just from experience,

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:14.510
right, troubleshooting
gets easier.

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:17.739
If you have a blueprint,
anytime an application--

00:13:17.739 --> 00:13:19.280
there's issue with
the communication,

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:22.670
you can look at the blueprint
and validate the flows are

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:23.420
allowed, you know?

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:26.420
Firewall rules are not
the cause of downtime.

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:29.460
The other thing is firewall
rules are a finite resource.

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:33.330
So it is important to plan
them before you start adding.

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:34.460
And we talked about tags.

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:36.126
I'm going to just
show an example of how

00:13:36.126 --> 00:13:39.410
you can reduce the number of
firewall rules just using tags.

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:44.190
So in this diagram, on the
right is my management subnet.

00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:45.770
There are my management VMs.

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:48.470
That will have access to
all the instances, right?

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:51.590
So if you look at the second
rule below that I have written,

00:13:51.590 --> 00:13:54.860
my source is going to
be management subnet.

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:56.920
Then my destination
is all instances.

00:13:56.920 --> 00:13:59.880
So, actually, from the
drop down in the console,

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:01.970
you can see all
instances in the network.

00:14:01.970 --> 00:14:03.660
And then you allow
specific ports.

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:05.390
In this case, I
want to allow a SSH,

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:07.490
and then I also
want to allow ICMP.

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:11.120
So now the next thing is
I have web servers, right?

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:14.570
I will group all the web servers
together based on function

00:14:14.570 --> 00:14:15.900
and add a tag www.

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:20.090
And then these web servers
want to talk to app servers

00:14:20.090 --> 00:14:21.620
over specific ports.

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:23.810
And I will add a
rule saying, all

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:27.910
VMs with the tag www--
those are my source--

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:31.149
can access VMs with a tag app.

00:14:31.149 --> 00:14:32.690
And then the access
should be allowed

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:34.250
for these ports and
these protocols--

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:37.280
in this case, http and https.

00:14:37.280 --> 00:14:38.990
STEPHANIE WONG:
So outside of just

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:41.480
the limitation of
firewall rules,

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:44.270
are there other reasons why
tagging in these firewall rules

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:45.182
would be a benefit?

00:14:45.182 --> 00:14:45.890
BINAL SHAH: Yeah.

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:47.990
Tagging definitely
helps you group

00:14:47.990 --> 00:14:49.235
together similar instances.

00:14:49.235 --> 00:14:52.634
So another application
comes on board,

00:14:52.634 --> 00:14:54.800
and you have the same rules,
you already allowed it.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:57.492
You just have to give the right
tag to the instances, right?

00:14:57.492 --> 00:14:58.700
So the rule is already there.

00:14:58.700 --> 00:14:59.810
You can reuse.

00:14:59.810 --> 00:15:02.510
And, remember, firewall
rules have a finite resource,

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:05.210
so you want to make
sure you efficiently

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:06.430
plan your firewall rules.

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:14.280
Firewall rules are also
important between VPC peerings.

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:20.820
So it's critical that you allow
only the flows that you want.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:23.010
Just a little bit on
VPC peering, right?

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:27.480
VPC peering-- within Google
Cloud, our VPC is global.

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:31.680
So if, within a VPC,
you had instances

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:35.320
running in US West and Europe,
these talk to each other.

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:37.860
So you do not need to peer
these two together, right?

00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:40.170
These are still
within the single VPC,

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:41.850
and then they can
talk to each other.

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:46.770
But there are use cases, like
SaaS services, like a large--

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:48.810
bees, right-- a
large organization

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:50.984
having multiple
departments, they

00:15:50.984 --> 00:15:52.650
have their different
projects, and, yet,

00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:54.120
they need some
communication to be

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.920
allowed between cross project.

00:15:56.920 --> 00:16:02.340
So that's where you peer VPCs,
and you peer between projects

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:04.410
or across organizations.

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:08.160
Now, when you peer, the routes
are exchanged between these two

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:08.880
VPCs, right?

00:16:08.880 --> 00:16:11.700
So in this example,
VPC enterprise 1

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:16.970
has a route to the subnet of
VPC SaaS via the peering link.

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:20.790
Vice versa, VPC SaaS has
access to enterprise subnets

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:23.200
via the VPC peering link.

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:25.920
So now it is the
firewall rules that

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:29.264
will control which instances
talk to which instances.

00:16:29.264 --> 00:16:30.180
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:31.804
So this is still
internal communication

00:16:31.804 --> 00:16:35.160
across separate VPCs,
yet you're still

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:37.800
able to maintain that
fine grained control using

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:38.680
these firewall rules.

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:39.346
BINAL SHAH: Yes.

00:16:39.346 --> 00:16:39.990
Absolutely.

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:42.550
And, yes, the communication
is not going out.

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:44.580
It is still within
the Google Cloud,

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:50.510
and the instance is able to talk
to each other using private IP.

00:16:50.510 --> 00:16:53.420
Keep in mind, though,
the firewall rules tables

00:16:53.420 --> 00:16:55.430
are not exchanged
between peers, and this

00:16:55.430 --> 00:16:57.950
is for security reasons.

00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:01.030
Your tags also will
not be transferred.

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:05.900
So you will use subnets as
source for the peer VPC.

00:17:08.500 --> 00:17:09.220
OK.

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:12.010
So we have the
firewall rules set up.

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:13.510
Next thing we need
to know is, when

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:15.190
you start deploying
in Cloud, you

00:17:15.190 --> 00:17:17.440
want visibility,
similar to what you have

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:19.480
on prem or other environments.

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:25.750
You want to be able to see what
is going in and out of your VM.

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:28.359
VPC flow logs enables that.

00:17:28.359 --> 00:17:31.280
It captures all flows
at the instance level,

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:33.130
so to and from the VM.

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:35.380
It is enabled at
the subnet level,

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:40.150
and then whenever a flow reaches
an instance or a flow goes out,

00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:43.240
be this between VM to
VM, or a VM talking to

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:46.550
on prem environment, or a
VM talking to the internet,

00:17:46.550 --> 00:17:48.400
you're able to
capture these flows.

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:51.800
Our aggregation is five
seconds, not in minutes.

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:54.940
So it is pretty real time.

00:17:54.940 --> 00:17:58.120
Some use cases for
VPC flow logs is,

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:00.160
when you are spinning
up a new environment,

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:01.430
you want to troubleshoot.

00:18:01.430 --> 00:18:04.240
So you go into flow logs
further as you need,

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:06.940
and you can check
whether the flows are

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:08.080
working as you expect.

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:08.996
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:18:08.996 --> 00:18:10.854
So following this idea
of your blueprint,

00:18:10.854 --> 00:18:13.270
you want to make sure that
everything's according to plan,

00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:15.370
according to your
security policies.

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:17.785
When you do look at these
logs, what kind of information

00:18:17.785 --> 00:18:19.480
does it actually capture?

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:21.980
BINAL SHAH: It actually captures
a rich set of information.

00:18:21.980 --> 00:18:25.420
So if I take any of
the flow log entry,

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:27.200
I would see connection details.

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:28.960
So who is my source IP?

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:29.960
Who is my destination?

00:18:29.960 --> 00:18:31.120
Who is it talking to?

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:33.260
What ports and
protocols are used?

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:35.290
And then it'll give me
details of the instance

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:38.050
that the flow is
occurring to or from.

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:40.240
So this is my source VM.

00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:41.140
This is the name.

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:43.510
This is the IP, the subnet,
VPC, and the project

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:45.220
that it lives in.

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:47.780
For the source,
that is external.

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:50.590
It will also give me what
ASN it's coming from,

00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:53.720
region and continent
details, et cetera.

00:18:53.720 --> 00:18:54.910
So it is pretty rich.

00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:57.250
I can actually take
all this information

00:18:57.250 --> 00:18:59.950
and put it into
BigQuery and generate

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:01.360
a lot of analytics out of it.

00:19:01.360 --> 00:19:03.730
I can actually generate
a networking dashboard.

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:06.350
Who are my top talkers?

00:19:06.350 --> 00:19:09.840
What is the bandwidth
utilization and egress charge?

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:12.890
So if I see egress by
zones, you know how much--

00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:14.659
if anybody looks
at their billing,

00:19:14.659 --> 00:19:15.700
it's sometimes confusing.

00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:18.610
This will give you
a chart and tell you

00:19:18.610 --> 00:19:20.080
how much egress per zone.

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:20.320
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:22.486
And that was going to be
my other follow up question

00:19:22.486 --> 00:19:23.910
is about exporting these logs.

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:25.390
You mentioned BigQuery.

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:29.020
If I wanted to export to my
CM and correlate these logs

00:19:29.020 --> 00:19:30.790
with other systems
that I'm using,

00:19:30.790 --> 00:19:32.110
would that be a possibility?

00:19:32.110 --> 00:19:33.130
BINAL SHAH: Absolutely.

00:19:33.130 --> 00:19:36.010
So all of our logs go
to Stackdriver Logging.

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:38.980
That's our tool
for Cloud Logging.

00:19:38.980 --> 00:19:41.920
And from here, you have the
capability to export it.

00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:45.970
So you can export
to Cloud storage.

00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:48.850
A lot of customers have
requirement to store logs

00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:50.449
for a certain amount of time.

00:19:50.449 --> 00:19:52.240
So you can just export
it to Cloud storage,

00:19:52.240 --> 00:19:53.920
and it will be there.

00:19:53.920 --> 00:19:56.210
Export it to BigQuery--
we just talked about it.

00:19:56.210 --> 00:19:57.970
A lot of rich information.

00:19:57.970 --> 00:20:00.080
And using our data
analytics platform,

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:03.930
it also integrates
with Data Studio,

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:06.730
which is our visualization
tool where we can create charts

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:07.810
at any time.

00:20:07.810 --> 00:20:09.040
And you mentioned CM--

00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:13.830
we can actually stream live
using Pub/Sub to any device

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:14.330
you want.

00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:17.230
So good use case for CM
is you want to correlate

00:20:17.230 --> 00:20:18.880
all the logs in one place.

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:21.040
So we have that
capability using Pub/Sub.

00:20:21.040 --> 00:20:22.248
STEPHANIE WONG: That's great.

00:20:24.015 --> 00:20:24.640
BINAL SHAH: OK.

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:28.150
So now you have your
applications front

00:20:28.150 --> 00:20:30.760
ended with Global
Load Balancer, and you

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:33.160
want to scale with it, right?

00:20:33.160 --> 00:20:36.940
So Global Load Balancer
provides us a lot of advantages.

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:39.660
So, first of all, you know,
when you're using application,

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:40.660
use TLS, right?

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:42.670
Wherever possible, use TLS.

00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:45.400
It increases the security,
and Google does not

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:47.260
charge extra for it.

00:20:47.260 --> 00:20:51.620
Now Global Load Balancer
is an AnyCast VIP,

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:53.650
so one VIP advertised globally.

00:20:53.650 --> 00:20:58.210
So you have one DNS
name mapping to one IP.

00:20:58.210 --> 00:21:02.020
And by virtue of our
global infrastructure,

00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:03.460
here I have an example.

00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:08.050
I have workloads in US
central, US west, US east,

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:09.590
as well as Asia, right?

00:21:09.590 --> 00:21:13.900
So my customers in Asia
can access the application

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:18.450
with a lot less latency
than if it were in US west.

00:21:18.450 --> 00:21:22.600
Now, Global Load Balancer
supports auto scaling.

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:23.780
What does that mean?

00:21:23.780 --> 00:21:26.440
If I have a region
seeing a lot of traffic,

00:21:26.440 --> 00:21:31.861
and I plan for the capacity
which is good for every day,

00:21:31.861 --> 00:21:34.360
and then, suddenly, there are
times, right, end of the year,

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:35.818
you have a sudden
spike in traffic.

00:21:35.818 --> 00:21:38.170
Black Friday, you have
sudden spike in traffic.

00:21:38.170 --> 00:21:40.840
You want your application
to be able to handle these.

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:44.850
Then the auto scaling feature
will be able to auto scale

00:21:44.850 --> 00:21:49.210
and add instances to absorb that
traffic and support your users.

00:21:49.210 --> 00:21:52.510
In addition, Global
Load Balancer also

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:54.610
can do cross region overflow.

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:57.550
So let's say, in US
east, you're suddenly

00:21:57.550 --> 00:22:03.700
seeing a lot of traffic, and
it may be unexpected, right?

00:22:03.700 --> 00:22:06.430
But by the time you figure
out what's going on,

00:22:06.430 --> 00:22:08.590
Global Load Balancer
is intelligent enough

00:22:08.590 --> 00:22:11.450
to divert the traffic to
the next closest region.

00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:13.660
So, in this case,
US central instances

00:22:13.660 --> 00:22:15.340
will start picking
up the traffic,

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:18.279
and your customers will not see
downtime, and that's important.

00:22:18.279 --> 00:22:19.570
STEPHANIE WONG: That's amazing.

00:22:19.570 --> 00:22:21.140
And this is all
supported, of course,

00:22:21.140 --> 00:22:23.950
by our large number of points
of presence and data centers

00:22:23.950 --> 00:22:25.150
globally.

00:22:25.150 --> 00:22:27.650
I see that our CDN is also
on this slide, as well.

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:30.280
How does this fit into
this overall picture?

00:22:30.280 --> 00:22:31.240
BINAL SHAH: Absolutely.

00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:35.180
So CDN enables you to
cache content at the edge.

00:22:35.180 --> 00:22:37.120
So you may have
frequent requests

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:38.620
for certain information.

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:40.170
You enable CDN.

00:22:40.170 --> 00:22:42.750
CDN integrates with
load balance-- http,

00:22:42.750 --> 00:22:44.980
https Load Balancer, right?

00:22:44.980 --> 00:22:48.190
So you enable CDN for
your back end service,

00:22:48.190 --> 00:22:51.640
and it'll start caching
content at the edge.

00:22:51.640 --> 00:22:54.940
So now when requests come in,
it'll be solved at the edge.

00:22:54.940 --> 00:22:57.240
Now you just offloaded
your back end service

00:22:57.240 --> 00:22:58.280
from a lot of requests.

00:23:02.560 --> 00:23:03.310
OK.

00:23:03.310 --> 00:23:07.060
Another solution that
we have is Cloud Armor.

00:23:07.060 --> 00:23:11.810
That provides DDOS protection at
the edge to your applications.

00:23:11.810 --> 00:23:15.700
Cloud Armor also integrates
with Global Load Balancer.

00:23:15.700 --> 00:23:17.620
Now, we know, right,
that instances are

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:19.390
protected by firewall rules.

00:23:19.390 --> 00:23:23.222
Cloud Armor works at the
edge to restrict access

00:23:23.222 --> 00:23:25.180
to the global VIP, because
the global VIP we'll

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:27.130
have a public IP.

00:23:27.130 --> 00:23:31.240
This way, here in
this example, I

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:34.290
have instances at the back end.

00:23:34.290 --> 00:23:36.320
They are protected
by firewall rules.

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:39.050
Remember, Global Load Balancers
are proxy load balances.

00:23:39.050 --> 00:23:41.734
So the source IP is
going to be different.

00:23:41.734 --> 00:23:43.150
It's going to be
the load balancer

00:23:43.150 --> 00:23:46.390
IPs, as seen from the
instance perspective, right?

00:23:46.390 --> 00:23:47.830
So, that traffic,
you want to make

00:23:47.830 --> 00:23:50.290
sure you only allow the
ports and protocols that you

00:23:50.290 --> 00:23:52.250
want to allow--

00:23:52.250 --> 00:23:54.340
the traffic you want to allow.

00:23:54.340 --> 00:23:57.470
Now, at the edge, the traffic
is coming from a client, right?

00:23:57.470 --> 00:23:59.650
So that's where
Cloud Armor comes in,

00:23:59.650 --> 00:24:03.700
and you're going to be
having control over which IPs

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:04.930
you're going to allow, right?

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:06.880
So in this case,
as an example, I'm

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:10.660
going to allow my corporate IPs
to access this load balancer.

00:24:10.660 --> 00:24:13.450
Or I may have specific customers
I have this application for,

00:24:13.450 --> 00:24:16.300
and I'll allow only their IPs.

00:24:16.300 --> 00:24:18.769
And then I can say,
deny everything else.

00:24:18.769 --> 00:24:20.560
STEPHANIE WONG: So this
is sort of speaking

00:24:20.560 --> 00:24:23.110
to that multilayer
defense where, of course,

00:24:23.110 --> 00:24:25.990
we provided DDOS protection
at the load balancer level,

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:28.330
but, from the edge,
we want to protect

00:24:28.330 --> 00:24:31.390
from even allowing traffic
from those source edge IPs.

00:24:31.390 --> 00:24:32.380
BINAL SHAH: Absolutely.

00:24:32.380 --> 00:24:34.171
STEPHANIE WONG: Now,
a lot of our customers

00:24:34.171 --> 00:24:37.150
I know also wonder about being
able to not only white list

00:24:37.150 --> 00:24:39.760
IPs, but also have the
ability to blacklist IPs.

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:42.149
Is that something that
Cloud Armor would enable?

00:24:42.149 --> 00:24:42.940
BINAL SHAH: It can.

00:24:42.940 --> 00:24:43.480
Absolutely.

00:24:43.480 --> 00:24:48.680
So I have my service open to
everyone, all who needs to.

00:24:48.680 --> 00:24:51.400
But I'm seeing-- like,
I'm seeing the logs,

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:53.980
and I'm suddenly seeing traffic
from somebody you don't want.

00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:55.570
And you're going to
find those IPs and say,

00:24:55.570 --> 00:24:56.830
I don't want this particular.

00:24:56.830 --> 00:24:57.560
I don't know.

00:24:57.560 --> 00:24:58.780
It's not my customer.

00:24:58.780 --> 00:24:59.770
It's not my corporate.

00:24:59.770 --> 00:25:01.340
I need to block this IP.

00:25:01.340 --> 00:25:06.300
So you can add rules to
your Cloud Armor policy

00:25:06.300 --> 00:25:09.370
specifically for that
IP or a set of IPs

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:10.769
and restrict that communication.

00:25:10.769 --> 00:25:11.560
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:25:11.560 --> 00:25:12.400
That's great.

00:25:12.400 --> 00:25:16.060
BINAL SHAH: So that's a great
way to block unwanted traffic.

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:20.150
All right.

00:25:20.150 --> 00:25:23.750
So here is an example
that shows I have my https

00:25:23.750 --> 00:25:27.140
Global Load Balancer
configured with the back ends,

00:25:27.140 --> 00:25:31.040
and I'm going to configure
Cloud Armor policy.

00:25:31.040 --> 00:25:36.260
Here, my corporate IP
range is 50.1.0.0/16,

00:25:36.260 --> 00:25:42.020
so I'm going to add a rule to
my policy, allow 50.1.0.0/16.

00:25:42.020 --> 00:25:45.020
Now, as you see, employees in
San Francisco and Singapore

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:48.520
are going to be able to
access the service fine,

00:25:48.520 --> 00:25:53.370
but you see someone with
an IP 120.1.1 is coming in.

00:25:53.370 --> 00:25:55.890
And that is not your employee.

00:25:55.890 --> 00:25:57.730
So that traffic
would be blocked.

00:25:57.730 --> 00:25:59.355
STEPHANIE WONG: Now,
one other question

00:25:59.355 --> 00:26:01.280
that I start to
think about is just

00:26:01.280 --> 00:26:04.850
the difference between setting
up Cloud Armor versus also

00:26:04.850 --> 00:26:07.730
setting up firewall rules
for your specific compute

00:26:07.730 --> 00:26:09.582
instances.

00:26:09.582 --> 00:26:10.290
BINAL SHAH: Yeah.

00:26:10.290 --> 00:26:13.050
So firewall rules protect
the compute instance.

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:14.970
So we will look at
this example, right?

00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:19.200
So my user comes in
with an IP 50.1.1.5

00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:21.840
and has access to the
Load Balancer VIP, right?

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:24.090
The load balancer is
a proxy load balancer,

00:26:24.090 --> 00:26:26.880
so it's going to
terminate that connection

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:29.200
and open a new connection
to the back end.

00:26:29.200 --> 00:26:31.800
So from my instance
perspective I

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:34.620
have the source as
the load balancer IP,

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:36.510
and the destination
is my instance.

00:26:36.510 --> 00:26:40.800
So the firewall rule will
have a source of destination--

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:46.320
source of the load balancer
IP and port-- let's say 4.4.3.

00:26:46.320 --> 00:26:48.390
Please allow this traffic.

00:26:48.390 --> 00:26:49.290
Anything else?

00:26:49.290 --> 00:26:51.040
Block that traffic.

00:26:51.040 --> 00:26:53.597
But in this case, if
I were not having--

00:26:53.597 --> 00:26:55.180
so this allowed, but
there is no way--

00:26:55.180 --> 00:26:58.490
my source will always be the
load balancer IP in this case.

00:26:58.490 --> 00:26:59.610
STEPHANIE WONG: That's
going to remain the same.

00:26:59.610 --> 00:27:00.318
BINAL SHAH: Yeah.

00:27:00.318 --> 00:27:02.220
So how do I protect
my front end?

00:27:02.220 --> 00:27:05.310
So Cloud Armor will come in
and say, OK, I'll protect you

00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:07.440
from the client IPs, right?

00:27:07.440 --> 00:27:10.921
So that's where I'm saying,
50.1.1.0/16 is what I'm going

00:27:10.921 --> 00:27:11.420
to allow.

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:11.880
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:27:11.880 --> 00:27:13.500
And that can vary so
much more in terms

00:27:13.500 --> 00:27:15.060
of the number of clients
that could be trying

00:27:15.060 --> 00:27:16.334
to access that load balancer.

00:27:16.334 --> 00:27:17.000
BINAL SHAH: Yes.

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:19.890
This can be globally.

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:21.230
STEPHANIE WONG: Great.

00:27:21.230 --> 00:27:22.790
Awesome.

00:27:22.790 --> 00:27:24.290
BINAL SHAH: All right.

00:27:24.290 --> 00:27:29.869
So I think let's try to look
at some of the configurations

00:27:29.869 --> 00:27:31.160
of what we talked about, right?

00:27:31.160 --> 00:27:32.493
We talked about a lot of things.

00:27:32.493 --> 00:27:35.720
How does it look in
Google Cloud console now?

00:27:35.720 --> 00:27:40.790
So I have some
instances created here.

00:27:40.790 --> 00:27:43.130
I have a management
instance, and I have

00:27:43.130 --> 00:27:46.020
assigned an external IP to it.

00:27:46.020 --> 00:27:47.960
Now, if you look at
the other instances,

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:50.240
they do not have external IP.

00:27:50.240 --> 00:27:53.390
I can try to go into
any one instance,

00:27:53.390 --> 00:27:56.851
and we will see
the configuration.

00:27:56.851 --> 00:27:57.350
Right?

00:27:57.350 --> 00:28:03.380
So my instance is in the US
west1 b zone, has a primary IP,

00:28:03.380 --> 00:28:05.300
does not have an external IP.

00:28:05.300 --> 00:28:08.300
I have also added a
tag to it saying www,

00:28:08.300 --> 00:28:11.420
if you remember our
previous example.

00:28:11.420 --> 00:28:12.020
OK.

00:28:12.020 --> 00:28:14.270
So now I want to SSH
into this instance.

00:28:19.280 --> 00:28:21.950
So let me SSH here.

00:28:21.950 --> 00:28:24.690
So I'm going to go into
my management instance,

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:26.980
and, from there, I can
access all the instances.

00:28:30.140 --> 00:28:32.160
I also have some
instance groups here.

00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:36.320
So we're going to walk through
a Global Load Balancer set up.

00:28:36.320 --> 00:28:38.787
My instance groups will
also have the same tags.

00:28:38.787 --> 00:28:41.120
So my template should have
the tags that I want to have.

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:44.490
OK.

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:45.870
So here I am.

00:28:45.870 --> 00:28:51.630
Now, I want to
SSH into 10.2.1.2.

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:55.080
So this is my internal
instance, right?

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:57.390
So, remember, I want
to be able to go out

00:28:57.390 --> 00:28:58.640
to get updates, et cetera.

00:28:58.640 --> 00:29:01.260
So I want to external
reachability.

00:29:01.260 --> 00:29:03.000
So how do I do that, right?

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:07.150
So I need to add Cloud NAT,
as we had discussed earlier.

00:29:07.150 --> 00:29:10.110
So to be able to
find Cloud NAT, you

00:29:10.110 --> 00:29:15.600
go into Products, Networking,
Network Services, Cloud NAT.

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:18.090
And I have a NAT gateway
configured already.

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:20.070
We just go through
the configuration.

00:29:20.070 --> 00:29:23.020
This is in my VPC, and
the region US west1,

00:29:23.020 --> 00:29:24.690
that's where my instances are.

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:27.990
And, here, I'm saying all
subnets primary and secondary

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:30.810
will be able to
access my Cloud NAT.

00:29:30.810 --> 00:29:33.780
I do have an option to say
specific subnets, right?

00:29:33.780 --> 00:29:36.900
So, here, I can
say Custom, Range,

00:29:36.900 --> 00:29:41.280
and then I would
say only my demo--

00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:44.212
my web servers would be
able to go out, right?

00:29:44.212 --> 00:29:44.920
Things like that.

00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:46.378
So I have the option
of doing that.

00:29:46.378 --> 00:29:48.630
I can also say,
only primary ranges.

00:29:48.630 --> 00:29:50.570
Subnets can't have
secondary ranges.

00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:52.860
But you may not want
those to go out.

00:29:52.860 --> 00:29:54.600
So you have control like that.

00:29:54.600 --> 00:29:56.710
I already have a
gateway connected,

00:29:56.710 --> 00:29:58.210
so let's go to my instance.

00:29:58.210 --> 00:30:00.910
And let's see if we
are able to go out.

00:30:00.910 --> 00:30:01.530
Yep.

00:30:01.530 --> 00:30:06.030
Without a public IP,
I am able to go out.

00:30:06.030 --> 00:30:08.260
So that's pretty neat.

00:30:08.260 --> 00:30:11.920
Now let's look at our
load balancer setup.

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:13.250
So this was my NAT setup.

00:30:13.250 --> 00:30:14.820
We already saw this.

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:16.960
We talked about VPC flow logs.

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:20.850
So I just want to walk through
one of the entry, how it looks,

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:22.860
and what information
you're able to see.

00:30:22.860 --> 00:30:23.910
So we did SSH.

00:30:23.910 --> 00:30:28.020
I am able to-- this is our
Stackdriver login screen.

00:30:28.020 --> 00:30:30.040
I am able to filter.

00:30:30.040 --> 00:30:33.750
So, here, I want to see who
all SSHed into my instance.

00:30:33.750 --> 00:30:37.380
So I will say destination port
22 and the IP of the instance

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:40.950
that I want to look into,
and here is a typical entry.

00:30:40.950 --> 00:30:44.790
So I have connection details,
the destination, the source--

00:30:44.790 --> 00:30:47.040
in this case, this
is my management VM--

00:30:47.040 --> 00:30:50.320
and the destination port is 22.

00:30:50.320 --> 00:30:53.211
So I know there
was an SSH action,

00:30:53.211 --> 00:30:55.460
and these are the details
on the destination instance.

00:30:55.460 --> 00:30:57.930
It tells me the instance
name and the region

00:30:57.930 --> 00:31:01.770
and the project that
the instance lives in.

00:31:01.770 --> 00:31:03.522
And of course,
there's a timestamp.

00:31:03.522 --> 00:31:04.980
And then there's
a source instance,

00:31:04.980 --> 00:31:07.530
details if the
source is within VPC,

00:31:07.530 --> 00:31:09.420
used by a similar instance.

00:31:09.420 --> 00:31:11.190
Details-- if it
is external, it'll

00:31:11.190 --> 00:31:13.800
give you more details on
the origin of the user.

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:17.550
Perfect.

00:31:17.550 --> 00:31:20.010
It also tells me
reporter's destination.

00:31:20.010 --> 00:31:24.670
That means the flow is
to my VM in this case.

00:31:24.670 --> 00:31:25.170
All right.

00:31:25.170 --> 00:31:29.100
Now let's quickly take a look at
the Global Load Balancer setup.

00:31:29.100 --> 00:31:31.330
So I have a load balancer setup.

00:31:31.330 --> 00:31:34.710
I have a global web, and
I have https enabled.

00:31:34.710 --> 00:31:36.570
And I will have
a back end to it.

00:31:36.570 --> 00:31:40.810
Now, I also have a Cloud
Armor policy configured here.

00:31:40.810 --> 00:31:44.640
So I go to Network
Security and Cloud Armor,

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:47.820
and then I have a policy
that will allow to everyone,

00:31:47.820 --> 00:31:51.320
but I know I don't want
to allow specific ranges,

00:31:51.320 --> 00:31:54.080
so I will have one
deny policy, and then

00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:56.100
I am OK to allow
to my corporate.

00:31:56.100 --> 00:31:58.300
In this case, I'm allowed
to the rest of the world.

00:31:58.300 --> 00:32:02.730
I can go in-- this is-- my
last rule is my default rule--

00:32:02.730 --> 00:32:05.490
I can go in and set it to deny.

00:32:05.490 --> 00:32:08.970
If I do deny, I do have
options to configure what kind

00:32:08.970 --> 00:32:11.040
of response the user will see.

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:15.510
So it can be 403, 404, or 502.

00:32:15.510 --> 00:32:17.940
So you have the flexibility
of configuring that.

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:19.356
STEPHANIE WONG:
So I see here this

00:32:19.356 --> 00:32:21.630
is how you white list versus
blacklist in IP ranges.

00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:24.120
BINAL SHAH: Absolutely.

00:32:24.120 --> 00:32:26.270
Now, let's look at
some of the logs here.

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:34.500
So I am going to try to send
this one request to the user,

00:32:34.500 --> 00:32:37.500
and I am able to see because
I have actually opened it up

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:38.730
to the world.

00:32:38.730 --> 00:32:46.850
And if I look at any one
particular log entry,

00:32:46.850 --> 00:32:49.970
you will go into
http load balancer.

00:32:49.970 --> 00:32:52.430
And then you see
enforce security policy.

00:32:52.430 --> 00:32:54.920
So it will tell you this rule--

00:32:54.920 --> 00:32:57.010
hit which policy rule, right?

00:32:57.010 --> 00:32:58.950
This flow, hit
which policy rule.

00:32:58.950 --> 00:33:01.670
So, in this case,
the security policy

00:33:01.670 --> 00:33:02.930
was configured to accept.

00:33:02.930 --> 00:33:08.150
The name of the policy was
cloud-armor-policy-glb-https.

00:33:08.150 --> 00:33:10.490
And the outcome was accept.

00:33:10.490 --> 00:33:12.110
Now, I could have
had a deny here,

00:33:12.110 --> 00:33:16.014
and it will show me which
policy was triggered.

00:33:20.370 --> 00:33:23.730
And here is the detail
of the forwarding rule

00:33:23.730 --> 00:33:28.600
that was tried to access and
then the zone configuration.

00:33:28.600 --> 00:33:30.950
All right?

00:33:30.950 --> 00:33:32.844
That's all I had.

00:33:32.844 --> 00:33:33.760
STEPHANIE WONG: Great.

00:33:33.760 --> 00:33:34.600
Thank you.

00:33:34.600 --> 00:33:37.386
I think it was really nice
to see it visually in terms

00:33:37.386 --> 00:33:39.010
of the flow logs
between either hitting

00:33:39.010 --> 00:33:41.050
the load balancer versus
your specific instances

00:33:41.050 --> 00:33:41.950
in the back end.

00:33:41.950 --> 00:33:43.150
BINAL SHAH: Yes.

00:33:43.150 --> 00:33:46.780
It is actually-- I would
highly encourage you, just go

00:33:46.780 --> 00:33:49.630
to Google Cloud, and
look at these options.

00:33:49.630 --> 00:33:53.380
Cloud Armor sits under Network
Security, Cloud NAT Load

00:33:53.380 --> 00:33:55.040
Balancing under
Networking Services.

00:33:55.040 --> 00:33:55.480
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:33:55.480 --> 00:33:56.896
I see it's giving
a lot of options

00:33:56.896 --> 00:33:59.080
for reducing that attack
surface by preventing you

00:33:59.080 --> 00:34:02.150
from always having any of your
instances open to the public.

00:34:02.150 --> 00:34:04.317
So there's a lot of options
here to protect our VMs.

00:34:04.317 --> 00:34:05.566
BINAL SHAH: And keep checking.

00:34:05.566 --> 00:34:07.840
We have a lot more features
coming out, so yeah.

00:34:07.840 --> 00:34:08.889
STEPHANIE WONG: Well,
thank you so much.

00:34:08.889 --> 00:34:11.500
We do have a couple of minutes
to just collect some questions,

00:34:11.500 --> 00:34:12.958
and we'll just be
back in a minute,

00:34:12.958 --> 00:34:14.679
and we'll try to
answer some of them.

00:34:14.679 --> 00:34:15.387
BINAL SHAH: Sure.

00:35:39.737 --> 00:35:40.820
STEPHANIE WONG: All right.

00:35:40.820 --> 00:35:41.390
Welcome back.

00:35:41.390 --> 00:35:43.610
We have a few
questions that we'd

00:35:43.610 --> 00:35:46.800
love to get answered for you
all that we've just collected.

00:35:46.800 --> 00:35:50.360
The first one, which is,
can I enforce firewall rules

00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:53.452
for other services
like App Engine?

00:35:53.452 --> 00:35:54.410
BINAL SHAH: So you can.

00:35:54.410 --> 00:35:57.730
App Engine firewalls use the
same firewall rules table,

00:35:57.730 --> 00:36:00.729
so firewall rules work
for GC, GKE, App Engine.

00:36:00.729 --> 00:36:01.520
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:36:01.520 --> 00:36:03.170
Great.

00:36:03.170 --> 00:36:06.110
And the next question
is, can I control

00:36:06.110 --> 00:36:09.710
communication between two
subnets using a third party

00:36:09.710 --> 00:36:10.686
firewall?

00:36:10.686 --> 00:36:12.310
BINAL SHAH: So this
is a good question.

00:36:12.310 --> 00:36:15.530
So when you said two
subnets, within a VPC,

00:36:15.530 --> 00:36:18.440
when you create subnets,
these are local subnets,

00:36:18.440 --> 00:36:21.680
and there will always be a
local route generated for that.

00:36:21.680 --> 00:36:26.510
So if I want to go to subnet
1, VPC 1, to subnet 2, VPC 2,

00:36:26.510 --> 00:36:30.690
VPC 1 is going to be the
local generated route.

00:36:30.690 --> 00:36:34.520
So this communication cannot
go to an external next hop

00:36:34.520 --> 00:36:36.090
and come back.

00:36:36.090 --> 00:36:38.570
However, between two VPCs--

00:36:38.570 --> 00:36:40.850
so a good use case for
a third party firewall

00:36:40.850 --> 00:36:43.010
is you have multiple
VPCs, and you

00:36:43.010 --> 00:36:46.310
want to see communication
between these two VPCs.

00:36:46.310 --> 00:36:48.590
That's when you use a
third party firewall.

00:36:48.590 --> 00:36:50.690
You spin up an instance
with multi NIC,

00:36:50.690 --> 00:36:53.210
and you can have
one NIC in each VPC.

00:36:53.210 --> 00:36:55.190
And, that way, your
next hop-- so when you

00:36:55.190 --> 00:36:57.920
want to communicate
with subnet 1 and VPC 1

00:36:57.920 --> 00:37:01.340
with subnet 1 and VPC 2,
can configure the next hop

00:37:01.340 --> 00:37:04.317
as the third party firewall,
and your communication

00:37:04.317 --> 00:37:06.400
would go through the
firewall so the firewall gets

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:07.650
the visibility it needs to get.

00:37:07.650 --> 00:37:07.910
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:37:07.910 --> 00:37:09.076
So there's two things there.

00:37:09.076 --> 00:37:11.690
You can set up multiple
NICs on a single VM,

00:37:11.690 --> 00:37:14.450
and then also that,
if you are using

00:37:14.450 --> 00:37:16.610
two subnets in the same
VPC, those are actually

00:37:16.610 --> 00:37:18.419
using local routes,
which is why you

00:37:18.419 --> 00:37:20.960
wouldn't be able to use a third
party firewall, in that case.

00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:21.668
BINAL SHAH: Yeah.

00:37:21.668 --> 00:37:23.510
So even if you
add a route, route

00:37:23.510 --> 00:37:25.820
can have priorities, like
even if you added out

00:37:25.820 --> 00:37:31.820
with a lower priority for two
subnets within the same VPC,

00:37:31.820 --> 00:37:33.950
that route is going
to be disabled.

00:37:33.950 --> 00:37:36.390
The local route will
always be preferred.

00:37:36.390 --> 00:37:36.890
I

00:37:36.890 --> 00:37:39.348
Do want to go back to your
point for third party firewalls.

00:37:39.348 --> 00:37:41.870
Yes, you need to
use a multi NIC VM.

00:37:41.870 --> 00:37:44.750
So a typical use case
for third party firewall

00:37:44.750 --> 00:37:46.770
is you have an
outside interface,

00:37:46.770 --> 00:37:48.870
and then you have at least
one inside interface.

00:37:48.870 --> 00:37:52.640
So you can have a multi NIC VM
with up to eight interfaces,

00:37:52.640 --> 00:37:54.890
and then you have the
inside configured and then

00:37:54.890 --> 00:37:58.369
the outside VPC so that
instance has a leg in each,

00:37:58.369 --> 00:38:00.410
and then the firewall
controls the communication.

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:01.326
STEPHANIE WONG: I see.

00:38:01.326 --> 00:38:02.370
OK.

00:38:02.370 --> 00:38:05.930
Our next question is, can I log
firewall rule activity, which

00:38:05.930 --> 00:38:08.420
I think we might have covered
in the presentation, but yes.

00:38:08.420 --> 00:38:09.503
BINAL SHAH: Actually, yes.

00:38:09.503 --> 00:38:11.300
You can log firewall
rules activity.

00:38:11.300 --> 00:38:13.310
So for each rule,
there's an option.

00:38:13.310 --> 00:38:16.524
You can enable logging, right?

00:38:16.524 --> 00:38:18.440
So all connections--
now, this is not sampled.

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:21.050
All connections would be logged.

00:38:21.050 --> 00:38:23.810
So it's a good use case
for troubleshooting.

00:38:23.810 --> 00:38:26.360
Also, when you suspect
something is going on,

00:38:26.360 --> 00:38:27.390
you can start logging.

00:38:27.390 --> 00:38:28.265
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:38:28.265 --> 00:38:30.230
So you want to
measure and keep a log

00:38:30.230 --> 00:38:32.240
of whether or not
things were accessed

00:38:32.240 --> 00:38:34.474
and whether those firewall
rules were being used.

00:38:34.474 --> 00:38:36.890
BINAL SHAH: And, again, these
logs go into our Stackdriver

00:38:36.890 --> 00:38:41.540
Logging, so you are able to see
it there, as well as export it

00:38:41.540 --> 00:38:42.879
to where we want.

00:38:42.879 --> 00:38:43.670
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:38:43.670 --> 00:38:45.530
Our next question
here is, when I

00:38:45.530 --> 00:38:49.340
block traffic using Cloud Armor,
what response code or message

00:38:49.340 --> 00:38:51.110
does the user receive?

00:38:51.110 --> 00:38:54.320
BINAL SHAH: So when you
configure a rule to deny,

00:38:54.320 --> 00:38:57.000
you have the option
to configure, what is

00:38:57.000 --> 00:38:58.700
the message the user will see?

00:38:58.700 --> 00:39:03.800
You can configure
a 404 error, 502,

00:39:03.800 --> 00:39:06.260
or you can say
forbidden, so 403.

00:39:06.260 --> 00:39:07.110
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:39:07.110 --> 00:39:08.485
I think we saw
that in your demo,

00:39:08.485 --> 00:39:10.460
as well, that you
get that dropdown.

00:39:10.460 --> 00:39:13.220
And our last one is,
can you elaborate

00:39:13.220 --> 00:39:15.830
on how you control
traffic between projects

00:39:15.830 --> 00:39:18.392
that are using a shared VPC?

00:39:18.392 --> 00:39:19.100
BINAL SHAH: Yeah.

00:39:19.100 --> 00:39:23.840
So shared VPC is a way to scale
with larger organizations,

00:39:23.840 --> 00:39:24.860
right?

00:39:24.860 --> 00:39:30.110
Typically, on prem, you have
network and security functions,

00:39:30.110 --> 00:39:33.020
so you have network
admin, security admins,

00:39:33.020 --> 00:39:36.360
and then there are
application web admins, right?

00:39:36.360 --> 00:39:38.690
Web admins don't
necessarily spin up subnets

00:39:38.690 --> 00:39:42.080
and add firewall rules,
where a network admin needs

00:39:42.080 --> 00:39:46.100
to have control
over the IPs that

00:39:46.100 --> 00:39:48.470
are being used across projects,
the security rules that

00:39:48.470 --> 00:39:50.000
are being added.

00:39:50.000 --> 00:39:51.770
So shared VPC enables that.

00:39:51.770 --> 00:39:53.690
So your networking
and security function

00:39:53.690 --> 00:39:55.520
is centralized in
the host project.

00:39:55.520 --> 00:39:57.240
That's where the VPC lives.

00:39:57.240 --> 00:40:00.560
Now, that VPC you shared with
different projects, service

00:40:00.560 --> 00:40:03.680
projects, so these are the
projects, like web projects,

00:40:03.680 --> 00:40:06.320
you connect to the
host VPC, and they're

00:40:06.320 --> 00:40:07.580
called service projects.

00:40:07.580 --> 00:40:10.310
So as you share subnets
with these service projects,

00:40:10.310 --> 00:40:13.250
the compute admin
over there will

00:40:13.250 --> 00:40:17.400
be able to spin up instances,
but not add firewall rules.

00:40:17.400 --> 00:40:19.190
So the firewall
rules function still

00:40:19.190 --> 00:40:22.040
stays in the host
project, and you still

00:40:22.040 --> 00:40:23.719
use firewall rules to do that.

00:40:23.719 --> 00:40:24.510
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:40:24.510 --> 00:40:26.570
So, again, you do
use firewall rules,

00:40:26.570 --> 00:40:28.964
even if you are
using shared VPC.

00:40:28.964 --> 00:40:29.630
BINAL SHAH: Yes.

00:40:29.630 --> 00:40:31.700
It is a single VPC still, right?

00:40:31.700 --> 00:40:32.960
It is one VPC.

00:40:32.960 --> 00:40:35.540
And you still use the
firewall rules just the way

00:40:35.540 --> 00:40:36.810
you use in other VPCs.

00:40:36.810 --> 00:40:37.790
STEPHANIE WONG: Great.

00:40:37.790 --> 00:40:37.950
All right.

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:39.870
So I think that's all
the questions we have.

00:40:39.870 --> 00:40:42.390
Thank you so much, Binal, again
for giving us this deep dive

00:40:42.390 --> 00:40:43.980
on security best practices.

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:46.200
I've truly enjoyed
going more in-depth

00:40:46.200 --> 00:40:48.437
about security and networking.

00:40:48.437 --> 00:40:49.770
BINAL SHAH: Thank you very much.

00:40:49.770 --> 00:40:51.010
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah,
thank you so much.

00:40:51.010 --> 00:40:52.470
So please, again, stay tuned.

